If three neighbourhood systems have computable presentations, there are computable presentations of the function space from their product into the third system and of the iterated function space such that the neighbourhood relation of the curry combinator is recursively decidable, and hence the combinator is a computable map.

The inner function-space presentation is built from the second and third presentations, the domain presentation from the product presentation and the third, and the codomain presentation from the first and the inner presentation, using the inclusion, equality, and consistency data of those presentations. For these presentations the curry relation is recursively decidable, and that decidable predicate is recursively enumerable.
